## Why This Is Asked
This question exams diagnosing subtle cross-region telemetry issues tied to time synchronization, control-plane stability, MTU handling, and NIC behavior, not just data-path faults.

## Key Concepts
- Time synchronization protocols (NTP/PTP)
- EVPN route convergence and BFD timers
- UDP telemetry MTU/fragmentation implications
- NIC offloads and timestamp behavior

## Code Example
```javascript
// Example: quick log parser for interval spikes
const fs=require('fs');
const lines=fs.readFileSync(process.argv[2],'utf8').split('\n');
let spikes=0;
lines.forEach(l=>{ if(/telemetry.*tail/.test(l)) spikes++;});
console.log('spikes',spikes);
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you automate this diagnostic with a runbook?
- What thresholds would trigger configuration changes (timers, MTU, pacing)?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests practical diagnostic reasoning for common cross-region latency issues, focusing on DNS routing, TLS termination, and edge pathing rather than code changes.

## Key Concepts

- DNS routing and TTL effects
- TLS handshake timing across regions
- Edge NAT/firewall behavior and LB logs
- Basic network measurement tools (dig, curl, mtr, openssl)

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example: measure DNS and HTTP timings in Node.js
const https = require('https');
const start = Date.now();
https.get('https://api.example.com/ping', res => {
  res.resume();
  res.on('end', () => console.log('elapsed', Date.now() - start));
});
```

## Follow-up Questions

- What are trade-offs between low vs high DNS TTL in this setup, and how would you monitor them?
- How would you validate TLS termination consistency across regions without changing apps?
